            <ArticleTitle>Standing laparoscopic herniorrhaphy in stallions using cylindrical polypropylene mesh prosthesis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>91-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Standing laparoscopic herniorrhaphy was performed in 9 stallions. Appropriate analgesia was achieved by sedation with detomidine and local flank infiltration with mepivacaine. Three portal sites at the paralumbar fossa were used to perform the herniorrhaphy by means of triangulation. A cylindrical polypropylene mesh was inserted and fixated in the inguinal canal. Subsequent adhesion formation resulted in an obliterated inguinal canal within 2 weeks. This minimal invasive technique allowed us to perform a testis sparing herniorrhaphy in the standing horse.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
